    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Stated INSTEAD of unique factorisation itself, which is the concept it came from: the kernel has no list, multiset or finite-product vocabulary, so `n is a product of primes, unique up to order` cannot be written in its language at all today. Euclid's lemma is the mathematical content of the uniqueness half and IS expressible. Reachable from the stated dependencies: gcd(p, a) divides p, so it is 1 or p, and the Bezout certificate turns the first case into p | b. formal.statement is written in the SAME core rendering the kernel emits for its own theorems, and uses ONLY symbols `build_nat_prelude` actually declares (checked against the 119-theorem inventory dumped on 2026-08-14), so it can be dispatched without inventing vocabulary. Primality is spelled out inline because the prelude has no `Prime` predicate. Binders carry readable names rather than the kernel's `x0, x1, ...`; that is a pretty-printing difference only, and any admitted proof would render with positional names.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
